# Back-end API modules

Canonical example: `back-end/src/modules/requests/` (`requests.routes.ts`, `requests.service.ts`, `requests.schema.ts`).

## Layers

- `*.routes.ts`: an exported `create<Feature>Router(db, now?)` factory. Only reads `req`, calls the service, and sets the status code. No SQL here.
- `*.service.ts`: business logic and SQL. Takes `db`, the `Employee` from `employeeOf(res)`, and `now`.
- `*.schema.ts`: Zod schemas for request bodies and query filters, parsed with `parseInput` from `shared/validate.ts`.
- Register new routers in `src/app.ts`. Any route mounted after `requireAuth` is private.

## Validation

```ts
// ❌ manual checks scattered in the route
if (typeof req.body.text !== "string" || !req.body.text.trim()) throw new HttpError(400, "...");

// ✅ schema + parseInput; the first Zod issue message becomes the 400 response
const { text } = parseInput(requestMessageSchema, body, "Mesaj boş olamaz.");
```

- Every Zod message is a Turkish sentence shown to the employee.
- Take limits, statuses, priorities, categories, and MIME types from `src/config/constants.ts`. Do not hard-code them in schemas.

## Errors and handlers

- Throw `HttpError(status, "Türkçe mesaj")`; `middleware/errorHandler.ts` turns it into `{ error }`. Unknown errors become a generic 500.
- Use 404 (not 403) for records that belong to another employee, as `parseId` and `loadDetail` do.
- Express 4 does not catch rejected promises. Keep handlers synchronous (`node:sqlite` is sync). If you ever need `async`, wrap it and forward errors with `next(error)`.

## When you add or change an endpoint

1. Update `src/docs/openapi.ts` (paths, schemas, limits, error responses).
2. Add the path to the Swagger coverage list and a behavior test in `test/api.test.ts`.
3. Do not add code to the legacy root files in `src/` (`domain.ts`, `db.ts`, `http.ts`, `sql.ts`, `constants.ts`, `assistant.ts`).
